= Future Work <FutureWork>
The DES framework establishes a scalable foundation for evaluating nationwide EV charging behavior in Denmark, but several deliberate abstractions were made to preserve computational feasibility. Future iterations should progressively relax these constraints to close the gap with real-world operational complexity. Furthermore, a more exhaustive grid search or random search over a wider parameter range should be conducted, as better configurations may exist beyond the current 252-point search. The baseline could also be reconsidered, as the current deviation-only heuristic with *$w_"dev"$* $= 100$ represents an extreme configuration that may not reflect how an unoptimized driver would realistically behave.

== Dynamic Traffic and Road Infrastructure
As described in @simulation_model, the current implementation queries OSRM on a static road network snapshot, assuming deterministic traversal under ideal conditions. This directly limits the accuracy of the path deviation cost $C_"dev"$, and integrating a dynamic traffic layer @sumo2018 sourced from real-time national road authority feeds would allow routing to reflect realistic speed variations throughout the simulated day. Combined with the existing exponential reevaluation schedule, this would create a more rigorous test of mid-journey adaptation. Road closures and maintenance windows would further stress-test system resilience under topological disruptions.

== Environmental and Seasonal Effects
As noted in @simulation_model, meteorological and thermal factors are deliberately omitted. Precipitation, headwinds, and sub-zero temperatures would increase energy draw per kilometer, shrinking the reachable station set for a given SoC and making range anxiety triggers more frequent. Cold-gating, where low battery temperatures limit charging power, would also meaningfully alter session durations and queue projections @tomaszewska2019. Beyond weather, the gravity model currently only reflects weekday commuting. Incorporating seasonal calibration with holiday travel could expose coverage gaps that standard workday simulations would not reveal.

== Infrastructure Fidelity and Live Data Integration
As described in @data_acquisition, station configurations are synthetically derived, meaning the model cannot faithfully represent individual stations. Interfacing with live APIs from charging network providers would replace these assigned configurations with real charger types, single or dual, and their corresponding power outputs, allowing the projected wait time $C_"wait"$ to be validated against real-world data. Expanding scope to model how the emergent charging distribution affects power grid stability during peak hours @ibrahim2024 would further expose a dimension that remains uncharacterized under the current model.

== System improvements
A compelling alternative to the vehicle-driven approach is a hybrid communication model. Instead of EVs pulling state data from stations, the stations could actively inform the EVs that hold reservations there when significant changes occur such as an unexpected queue surge allowing them to reconsider their choice.

Furthermore, the scoring system could benefit from additional metrics, such as charger occupancy duration on the station side, and energy cost paid relative to agent preference on the EV side, which would provide a more complete picture of system performance.